{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 111 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "f180af9760e44b2902ffcc5e1b95bc79875801dac589a21c20073627bad94905" :edb/db-before-t 110 :edb/db-after-t 111 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533423 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:46:09.055Z" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186055304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186053031 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186055304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329511 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186055304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186055304 1071 "For which values of $x$ is $x^{2} - 5x > 0$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186055304 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-5x>0?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186055304 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x & >0 \\\\ x(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ \nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-5x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059649 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329720 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059649 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329724 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329719 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329722 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329725 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059681 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329852 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329851 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329680 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186060287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058314 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186060287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329191 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186060287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186060287 1071 "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186060287 1071 "What is $\\sin (x - \\frac{π}{4})$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186060287 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329911 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329912 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329913 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329914 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329917 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329940 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064160 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329944 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329889 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329926 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329927 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064224 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329728 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329932 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329952 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329839 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329844 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329845 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329892 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329895 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329888 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329902 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064319 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329954 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329868 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064323 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329869 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064376 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329941 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329899 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329900 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064389 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329949 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064389 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329956 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062905 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329266 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064813 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x = 3$?\n$f(x) = {x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 3; 2x + 3, \\mid x > 3$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064813 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<3 \\\\ 0, & x=3 \\\\ 2x+3, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064813 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062947 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329493 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064820 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1, 5]$?\n\n1. $f(x) = x^{3}$\n2. $g(x) = \\mid x \\mid$\n3. $h(x) = \\ln (x - 1)$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064820 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5]?$\n\n- $f(x)={x}^{3}$\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064820 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{3}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[1,5]$ and differentiable on $(1,5)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$ but this point does not lie inside $(1,5).$\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ is not defined at $x=1$, so it is not continuous at $x=1$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,5].$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062983 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329523 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064826 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[- 1, 1]$?\n\n1. $g(x) = \\mid x \\mid$\n2. $s(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$\n3. $h(x) = 2x$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064826 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x|$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $h(x)=2x$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064826 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not continuous at $x=0$, which means it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function III satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063007 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329272 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064830 1071 "![](images/42/429c0a3eb7a6b1b0a99fba09eb7dd062f645669e99d39a919dc369467b4c373d.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[- 2, 4]$ and differentiable on $(-2, 4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c ∈ (-2, 4)$ such that $\\left.\\right|_______$.*" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064830 1071 "![](images/42/429c0a3eb7a6b1b0a99fba09eb7dd062f645669e99d39a919dc369467b4c373d.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-2,4]$ and differentiable on $(-2,4).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-2,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064830 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-2$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-2)}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-2,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{3}.$*" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063031 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329370 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064834 1071 "The function $U(t)$ models the number of users of a new mobile phone app, $t$ hours after launching the app. What is the best interpretation of $U^{′}(4) = 650$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064834 1071 "The function $U(t)$ models the number of users of a new mobile phone app, $t$ hours after launching the app. What is the best interpretation of ${U}^{′}(4)=650?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064834 1074 "The function ${U}^{′}(t)$ represents the rate at which the number of users is changing at the moment $t$ hours after launch. \n\nTherefore, ${U}^{′}(4)=650$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *$4$ hours after the launch, the number of users increased at a rate of $650$ users per hour*" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063061 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329535 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064839 1071 "Find the derivative of $y = \\sin (\\cos x)$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064839 1071 "Find the derivative of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064839 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063067 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329231 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064840 1071 "Consider a rectangle such that the height is fixed but its base $b$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of $A^{′}(b)$ if $A$ is the area of the rectangle?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064840 1071 "Consider a rectangle such that the height is fixed but its base $b$ can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${A}^{′}(b)$ if $A$ is the area of the rectangle?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064840 1074 "The function $A(b)$ gives the area of a rectangle with base $b.$ So\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(b)=\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}b}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }A}{\\text{Change in }b}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}^{′}(b)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the base changes." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063073 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329537 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064841 1071 "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y = f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n1. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x = 2$\n2. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x = 2$\n3. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x = 2$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064841 1071 "![](images/bb/bb17384f2313820f526f1ba4356f6f3c9e11fbb3791e4f7e2c6044cc604bf662.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064841 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of $f(x)$ has a has a sharp edge (cusp) at $x=2.$  Therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)$ does not exist and hence it can't be equal to zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since $f(x)\\le f(2)$ for $x$ near the point $x=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=2$ since the derivative does not exist at this point." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063115 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329501 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064848 1071 "![](images/2c/2c735c35700b6cda3aeb608c97d36871eed46db48e9c77f9f118db6a8f09383a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* $f^{′}(x) < 0$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186064848 1071 "![](images/2c/2c735c35700b6cda3aeb608c97d36871eed46db48e9c77f9f118db6a8f09383a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186064848 1074 "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](images/8c/8cf8b0b8ea68a7fcaac0456770dab60bf124721897e65a95701ec928730e388e.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave down ($⌢$) at $x=e$ only." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329768 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329769 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329865 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329866 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329867 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329859 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329860 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329861 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329862 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068703 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329863 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186068704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329885 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067366 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186069371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329541 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069371 1071 "Select the function that is continuous at $x =-2$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186069371 1071 "Select the function that is continuous at $x=-2.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069371 1074 "The correct answer is shown below. Despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=1$, the graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/63/63e7a219c0c9bf7917a094ac0e65e9d4f18eb855476830621700d58ac29ba1b0.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=-2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in the neighborhood of $x=-2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067372 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186069372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329363 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069372 1071 "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above.\n\n1. $lim_(x → 2^{-})f(x)$ is finite\n2. $lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x)$ is finite\n3. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x = 2$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186069372 1071 "![](images/8e/8eb53506d9e01d09fe3e072a2c6ed1ade05e1cc185693bdd66b34bdf75797ad9.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)$ is finite\n- $f(x)$  has a discontinuity at $x=2$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069372 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. We get ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function contains a break exactly at $x=2$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067396 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186069376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329471 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186069376 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=1,2$ and $4$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1,2,4.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329919 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329922 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329923 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329924 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078391 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329925 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329916 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078392 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329928 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329813 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329814 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329842 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329843 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329848 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329849 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078434 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329850 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329771 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329838 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078495 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329920 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329765 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329802 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329803 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078542 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329804 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329764 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329767 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329801 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078573 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329877 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078573 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329915 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329816 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329824 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078622 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329881 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078623 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329880 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078623 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329883 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078624 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329882 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076322 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078929 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329385 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078929 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{\\sqrt{x + 4} - 2}{x}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078929 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078929 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0+4}-2}{0}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+4}-2}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x+4-4}{x(\\sqrt{x+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0+4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076328 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329536 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078930 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → 1^{-})\\frac{x - 1}{\\sqrt{x + 3} - 2}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078930 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078930 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=1$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & =\\frac{1-1}{\\sqrt{1+3}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{x+3}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+3}-2)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{{(\\sqrt{x+3})}^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x+3-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{x+3}+2)}{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(\\sqrt{x+3}+2) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3}+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076340 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329626 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078932 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → 4)\\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 2}{x - 4}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078932 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{x-4}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078932 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{x-4} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4}-2}{4-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{x-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-2)(\\sqrt{x}+2)}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{x-4}{(x-4)(\\sqrt{x}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 4}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076382 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078939 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329460 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078939 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078939 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078939 1071 "What is $lim_(x → 0)\\frac{x}{7\\sin x}$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078939 1074 "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076424 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078946 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329497 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078946 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078946 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x>3.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078946 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{d}{dx}\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\cos t}{t^{2} + 4}dt$ for $x > 3$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078946 1074 "The function $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}$ is continuous on $[3,x],$ where $x>3.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3}^{x}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076448 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078950 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329575 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078950 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078950 1071 "For $x > 2$, find $f^{′}(x)$ if $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2 - t^{2}}dt$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078950 1071 "For $x>2$, find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078950 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076520 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329280 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078962 1071 "Find $F^{′}(0)$ if $F(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_{x}^{\\sin (x)}\\sqrt[3]{1 + t^{2}}dt$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078962 1071 "Find ${F}^{′}(0)$ if $F(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078962 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\sqrt[3]{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${F}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\sqrt[3]{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(0)}-\\sqrt[3]{1+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\sqrt[3]{1}-\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076586 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329412 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078973 1071 "The graph of the function $y = f(x)$ passes through $(2, 3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0, 3)$. If $f^{′}(2) =-1$, estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078973 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(2,3)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0,3).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=-1,$ estimate the value of $f(2.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078973 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(2,3),$ so we have $f(2)=3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(2)=-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =3+(-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =3-(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.2) & ≈L(2.2) \\\\  & =3-(2.2-2) \\\\  & =3-0.2 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(0,3),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=2,$ and therefore we have an underestimate." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076604 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329211 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078976 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a  function such that ${\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=3.$ What is the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078976 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a function such that $\\displaystyle \\int_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}f(x)dx =-1$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}f(x)dx = 3$. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{6}f(x)dx$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078976 1074 "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\frac{1}{5}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{6}^{3}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+5 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076634 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329437 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078981 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that $f^{′}(x) = x^{2} - 4$ and $f(4) = 13$. Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x = 4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078981 1071 "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-4$ and $f(4)=13.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=4$ to estimate the value of $f(3.5).$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078981 1074 "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(4)=13,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-4,$ then ${f}^{′}(4)={4}^{2}-4=12.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=13+12(x-4).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3.5)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.5.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.5) & ≈L(3.5) \\\\  & =13+12(3.5-4) \\\\  & =13+12(-0.5) \\\\  & =13-6 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076682 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329355 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078989 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{\\ln x}{x^{2}}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078989 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078989 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076694 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329610 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078991 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x}}{x}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078991 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078991 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076700 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329320 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078992 1071 "Evaluate $lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{e^{x}}{x^{9}}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078992 1071 "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{9}}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078992 1074 "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫{x}^{9}$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{9}}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076712 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329572 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078994 1071 "![](images/30/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y = f(x)$ above continuous?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078994 1071 "![](images/30/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078994 1074 "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-3$ and left-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-3,0]$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-3,0]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,1]$ and $[0,4]$ contain the endpoint $x=0,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076730 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329394 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078997 1071 "![](images/09/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous?\n\n1. $x ∈ (- ∞, 0]$\n2. $x ∈ [- 3, 2]$\n3. $x ∈ (0, ∞)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078997 1071 "![](images/09/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in [-3,2]$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078997 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-3,2].$ It is continuous on $(-3,2),$ and also right continuous at $x=-3$ and left continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(0,\\infty )$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076736 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329469 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078998 1071 "![](images/ff/ff6f5e2fbd530ee9d90c2adf02bb212886fb137c1d55c9e9d78958902ccc9f13.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous?\n\n1. $x ∈ (- ∞, 1]$\n2. $x ∈ [1, ∞)$\n3. $x ∈ (-1, 1)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186078998 1071 "![](images/ff/ff6f5e2fbd530ee9d90c2adf02bb212886fb137c1d55c9e9d78958902ccc9f13.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in [1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,1)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186078998 1074 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,1].$ Although the function is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1),$ it is not left-continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[1,\\infty ).$ It is continuous on $(1,\\infty )$ and also right continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $(-1,1).$ It is continuous on $(-\\infty ,1)$ and this contains the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076898 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186079025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329377 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079025 1071 "The population $p$, in millions, of a particular colony of bacteria after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $p(t) = \\frac{2^{t^{2} + 1}}{\\ln 2}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population as a function of $t$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186079025 1071 "The population $p$, in millions, of a particular colony of bacteria after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $p(t)=\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population as a function of $t?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079025 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change of the bacteria population is equal to ${p}^{′}(t).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot (2t)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =2t\\cdot {2}^{{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076922 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186079029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329600 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079029 1071 "The area of the rectangle is increasing. The base of the rectangle is $b$, and its height is $10$ times its base. Find the rate of change of the area of a rectangle with respect to its base." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186079029 1071 "The area of the rectangle is increasing. The base of the rectangle is $b,$ and its height is $10$ times its base. Find the rate of change of the area of a rectangle with respect to its base." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186079029 1074 "The area of a rectangle is given by  \n\n$$\nA=bh,\n$$\n\n where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the height of the rectangle.\n\nWe are given that $h=10b,$ so we can express the area $A$ as a function of the base $b$ by substituting $h=10b$ in the formula above. We get\n\n$$\nA=b(10b)=10{b}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $A(b)$  with respect to $b$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(b) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}(10{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =10\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}b}({b}^{2}) \\\\  & =10(2b) \\\\  & =20b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083239 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329787 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083240 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329788 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329782 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083241 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329789 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329792 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329793 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329794 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329795 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329797 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329798 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329783 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329784 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329796 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329799 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329800 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329790 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083254 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329791 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083302 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329903 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083335 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329945 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186081173 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186083712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329207 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186083712 1071 "The rolls of an unfair six-sided die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | 