splaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}x(t)y(t)dt$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134390 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Find $⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134390 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}]-[\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132355 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329432 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134391 1071 "Let $V = C[0, 2]$, the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 2]$. Find $⟨t, e^{t}⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)dt$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134391 1071 "Let $V=C[0,2],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2].$ Find $⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134391 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132397 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329371 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134398 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134398 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and 