3}$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134406 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+5{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134406 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (3\\cdot 3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132451 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329276 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134407 1071 "Let $V = R_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t) = 1 - 2t$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134407 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2)+x(3)y(3).\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $p(t)=1-2t.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134407 1074 "The norm of a vector $p(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=1-2t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=-1,\\,p(2)=-3,\\,p(3)=-5.\n$$\n\nComputing $‖p(t)‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{p(1)p(1)+p(2)p(2)+p(3)p(3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2}+[p(3)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{35}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132463 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329457 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134409 1071 "Let $V = C[π, 2π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[π, 2π]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{π}^{2π}x(t)y(t)dt$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t) = \\cos t$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134409 1071 "Let $V=C[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[\\pi ,2\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134409 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\,{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{4}){|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi }{4})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =(\\pi +0)-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡t=\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖f(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132469 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329219 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134410 1071 "Let $V = R_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2)$.\nNormalize the vector $p(t) = 2 + t$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134410 1071 "Let $V={R}_{1}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $1,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nNormalize the  vector  $p(t)=2+t.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134410 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖p(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨p(t),p(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nSince $p(t)=2+t,$ we obtain\n\n$$\np(1)=3,\\,p(2)=4.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),p(t)⟩ & =[p(1)]^{2}+[p(2)]^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖p(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{25}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $p(t)$, we divide $p(t)$ by its norm $‖p(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\frac{p(t)}{‖p(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132493 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329463 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134414 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1; \\sqrt{5}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}, & B|= & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0; 0\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}, & C|= & 4 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}4; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134414 1071 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134414 1074 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|B|=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-15\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has identical columns: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132529 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329382 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134420 1071 "Let $A$ be a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix such that $\\mid A|= 0$. Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $A$ has two identical rows\n2. One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n3. $A$ has two identical columns" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134420 1071 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n- $A$ has two identical columns" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134420 1074 "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132589 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329586 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134430 1071 "Given that $x, y ∈ C^{4}$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x, y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134430 1071 "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{4},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134430 1074 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+1\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-4})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{6}+3\\cdot (\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 6+3\\cdot (2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(2+2i)+(-4)+12+(6-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =16-i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132595 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329233 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134431 1071 "Given that $x, y ∈ C^{3}$, compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x, y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 - \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 + \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134431 1071 "Given that $x,y\\in {C}^{3},$ compute the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨x,y⟩$ of the following two vectors.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2-i\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1+i \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2+i\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134431 1074 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}} \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{1+i})+2\\cdot \\overset{―}{3}+(2-i)(\\overset{―}{2+\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =(1+i)\\cdot (1-i)+2\\cdot 3+(2-i)(2-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =2+6+(3-4\\text{i}) \\\\  & =11-4i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132631 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329604 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134437 1071 "![](images/5a/5aaf29ab4dd2de3d104ca37a71ee66078f985b908465a77ca06ba8ffadd50210.png)\n\nIn the diagram above,  $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$, while ${i}^{′}$ and  ${j}^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T.$\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134437 1071 "![](images/5a/5aaf29ab4dd2de3d104ca37a71ee66078f985b908465a77ca06ba8ffadd50210.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $i$ and $j$ are the vectors of the standard basis of $R^{2}$, while $i^{′}$ and $j^{′}$ are their images under the linear transformation $T$.\nWhat is the standard matrix of $T$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134437 1074 "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n${i}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132637 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329321 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134438 1071 "An inner product on $V = R^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 3x_{3}y_{3}$.\nCalculate the norm of the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134438 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \nCalculate the norm of the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134438 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $‖v‖$ using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132643 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329405 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134439 1071 "Let $V = C[0, 1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 1]$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$.\nIf $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t^{2} + k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134439 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)={t}^{2}+k$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134439 1074 "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t({t}^{2}+k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}+kt)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{k}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132649 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329525 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134440 1071 "Let $V = C[- π, π]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[- π, π]$, equipped with the inner product\n$⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{- π}^{π}f(t)g(t)dt$.\nGiven the functions $f(t) = t$ and $g(t) = \\sin t$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 2π$\n2. $⟨f(t), g(t)⟩ = 0$\n3. $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134440 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=2\\pi $\n- $⟨f(t),g(t)⟩=0$\n- $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134440 1074 "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\pi +\\pi )+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132655 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329343 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134441 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134441 1071 "Let $V = R^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 2x_{2}y_{2} + 3x_{3}y_{3}$.\nIf $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨ \\cdot, \\cdot ⟩$ in $V$, then $k =$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134441 1071 "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134441 1074 "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot (-1\\cdot k)+3\\cdot (2\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =-2-2k+18 \\\\  & =16-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 16-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132888 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329242 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134480 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = (x + 1)^{2} - 4$. For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x) = k$ has a solution on the interval $x ∈ [- 4,-2]$?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134480 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]?$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134480 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,-2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-4)$ and $f(-2).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=5$ and $f(-2)=-3,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-3,5].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=2$ belongs to the interval $[-3,5].$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132894 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329258 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134481 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q$?\n\n1. $Q^{T}$ is orthogonal\n2. The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n3. $2Q$ is orthogonal" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186134481 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\n- ${Q}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n- $2Q$ is orthogonal" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186134481 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The columns of the identity matrix are orthonormal. Therefore, $I$ is an orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Computing the product $2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186161971 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329701 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186161972 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329702 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186162560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186160622 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186162560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329499 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186162560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186162560 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 0 & 19 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations \n\n- ${R}_{1}:=…$\n- ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}$\n- ${R}_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form ${R}_{i}:={R}_{i}+k{R}_{2},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the missing elementary row operations?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186162560 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 0 & 19 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $B$ above. By applying three matrix elementary row operations\n\n1. $R_{1}:=…$\n2. $R_{3}:=R_{3} + 4R_{2}$\n3. $R_{4}:=…$\n\neach of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i} + kR_{2}$, the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B$. What are the missing elementary row operations?" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186162560 1074 "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries above and below ${a}_{22}=-1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 0 & 19 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 0 & 19 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing elementary row operations are\n\n$\\,$ ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+5{R}_{2}.$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186166856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329673 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175907 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329846 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329857 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175909 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329858 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329759 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175928 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329763 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329942 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186175945 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329943 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329694 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329698 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176057 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329822 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176061 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329703 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176102 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329835 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175102 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329423 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176499 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1, & x<0.5 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2}, & x\\ge 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $(0,1)$\n- $[0,2]$\n- $[1,2]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176499 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n$f(x) = {x - 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0.5 \\\\ 4 - x^{2},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0.5$.\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n1. $(0, 1)$\n2. $[0, 2]$\n3. $[1, 2]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176499 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(0,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(x-1)=(0.5)-1=-0.5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(4-{x}^{2})=4-(0.5)^{2}=3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0.5$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.5.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[1,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0.5,$ which is not in the interval $[1,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175108 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329443 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176500 1071 "![](images/2a/2ae902b1cccc00b59401ecba306ef7f6f47e92f6439614c13c349283d9e763ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)$?\n\n1. $[- 1, 2]$\n2. $(1, 3]$\n3. $[- 4,-1]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176500 1071 "![](images/2a/2ae902b1cccc00b59401ecba306ef7f6f47e92f6439614c13c349283d9e763ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[-4,-1]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176500 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,2]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ So, it can't be continuous at this point. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-4,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-4,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175120 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329309 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176502 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y = \\log_{2} x$?\n\n1. $(0, 5]$\n2. $[1, 5]$\n3. $[0, 5]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176502 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\n- $(0,5]$\n- $[1,5]$\n- $[0,5]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176502 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175126 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329582 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176503 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ -x+2, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n- $[0,2]$\n- $(1,2)$\n- $[-2,2]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186176503 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n$f(x) = {x^{2} + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ -x + 2,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$.\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\n1. $[0, 2]$\n2. $(1, 2)$\n3. $[- 2, 2]$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186176503 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(-x+2)=-1+2=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- The interval $(1,2)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Finally, consider $[-2,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=1,$ which is belongs to the interval $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-2,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186184145 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329762 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186184226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329884 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186184340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329770 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186188228 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329721 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186193341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186191943 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186193341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329500 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186193341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186193341 1071 "Consider the curve $x + \\ln y = 4$. Given that $x = x(t)$, $y = y(t)$, and that $\\frac{dx}{dt} =-1$ on the curve at the point where $x = 4$, find the value of $\\frac{dy}{dt}$ at that point." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186193341 1071 "Consider the curve $x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=4.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ on the curve at the point where $x=4,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186193341 1074 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ and $x=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=4$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ 4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186217158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329777 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186234084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329486 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186234084 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\n- $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\n- $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186234084 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $7,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$\nII. - $P-Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $4$\nIII. - $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$" 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186234104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329579 13194139533423 17592186045585 true] [17592186234104 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{'}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day." 13194139533423 17592186045585 false] [17592186234104 1074 "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{′}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{ali